{
  "summary": "Fixes Tapscript Miniscript duplicate key checking in descriptors by comparing keys as x-only public keys instead of full 33-byte compressed keys. In Tapscript context, keys sharing an x-coordinate but differing in parity byte are now detected as duplicates, causing `DuplicateKeyCheck()` to fail and reject the descriptor as not sane. P2WSH descriptor parsing remains unchanged and continues to distinguish full 33-byte keys.",
  "problem": "When a `tr()` Miniscript descriptor uses keys that differ only by their parity prefix, `KeyParser::KeyCompare` treats them as distinct 33-byte keys and accepts the descriptor as sane. On-chain, Tapscript discards parity so both branches evaluate using identical x-only public keys, violating BIP-379 duplicate key invariants and allowing third-party transaction malleability.",
  "evidence": [
    "PR description and commit message cite issue #36414: 'In Tapscript Miniscript descriptors (inside tr()), public keys serialize as 32-byte x-only pubkeys... KeyParser::KeyCompare compared keys as full 33-byte CPubKeys, causing keys that differ only in parity... to be treated as distinct.'",
    "BIP-379 malleability issue cited: 'BIP-379 malleability analysis relies on the invariant that no public key is repeated. Because one signature satisfies both branches, a third party can malleate transactions by flipping the branch selector without possessing the private key.'"
  ],
  "dependencies": {
    "depends_on": [],
    "enables": []
  },
  "scope_notes": "Touches descriptor key comparison and Miniscript duplicate sanity checks, with unit tests added to descriptor and miniscript test suites.",
  "changed_since_previous": "",
  "card": "This pull request modifies Tapscript Miniscript descriptor parsing to compare public keys as 32-byte x-only keys rather than 33-byte compressed keys during duplicate key checks. Previously, keys with the same x-coordinate but differing parity prefixes passed duplicate checks in tr() descriptors even though they resolve to identical public keys on-chain. This violated BIP-379 duplicate key invariants and allowed third parties to malleate transactions by switching branches without new signatures. Segwit v0 descriptors (wsh()) retain full 33-byte key comparisons.",
